Frontend Rules (Next.js + Tailwind + shadcn/ui + Plotly)

Tech stack
	•	Framework: Next.js (App Router) with TypeScript. Use Server Components by default; mark client parts with "use client".  ￼
	•	Styling: Tailwind CSS.
	•	UI kit: shadcn/ui (built on Radix Primitives). Prefer these components for a11y and consistency.  ￼ ￼
	•	Charts: plotly.js via react-plotly.js. Input is Plotly JSON from the backend (data, layout, frames, config).  ￼ ￼
	•	Data fetching / cache: TanStack Query (React Query). Use queries for reads, mutations for writes.  ￼
	•	Design method: Atomic Design for components (atoms → organisms → pages).  ￼ ￼

	•	Keep top-level pages thin: compose organisms; no data shaping in pages.

Data & API
	•	All reads use React Query with typed fetchers (lib/api.ts).
	•	Default staleTime 60s for GETs; set retry: 1.
	•	Handle errors with a shared <ErrorState/> component (retry button calls refetch()).

Plotly rendering (critical path)
	•	Use a single component components/PlotlyFigure.tsx:
	•	Props: src: string (endpoint that returns pio.to_json), optional height.
	•	Fetch the URL, JSON.parse the response, pass to <Plot /> with useResizeHandler, style={{ width:'100%', height:'100%' }}.
	•	Do not rebuild charts on the client; the server owns figure generation.  ￼ ￼

UI conventions
	•	Use shadcn/ui + Radix for Select, Tabs, Dialog, Tooltip, Table.
	•	Accessible labels & aria attributes are mandatory; Radix provides primitives but you must supply labels.  ￼
	•	Tables: sticky header, zebra rows, sortable columns; collapse to Cards on mobile.

Theming
	•	Colors from the DA:
	•	Primary navy for backgrounds, electric green for “good”, orange for highlights.
	•	Implement as Tailwind CSS variables under :root (light) and .dark (if needed).

Pages (MVP scope)
	•	/rankings: filters (league, season, metric, min minutes) + toggle Table/Chart. Chart uses /charts/rankings/bar.
	•	/scatter: controls (x metric, y metric, league, season, min minutes, position) + /charts/scatter/metrics.
	•	/players/[id]: player header, metric bars, “similar players” list.
	•	/compare: two autocompletes + side-by-side bars.

Performance
	•	Prefer Server Components; isolate interactive bits as Client Components.  ￼
	•	Memoize heavy client widgets; keep Plotly in a client component only.
	•	Add Cache-Control on chart endpoints so React Query + browser cache work together.

Accessibility
	•	Keyboard navigable controls; visible focus states.
	•	Tooltips mustn’t hide essential info.
	•	Provide text alternatives for icons; use aria-label on icon-only buttons.  ￼

Testing & quality
	•	TypeScript strict mode.
	•	ESLint + Prettier on commit.
	•	Add simple Playwright smoke tests for the 4 MVP pages (page renders, filters change URL/search params, chart loads).

Definition of Done (per PR)
	•	Follows folder conventions & Atomic layers.  ￼
	•	Uses shadcn/ui/Radix components (no raw HTML controls unless justified).  ￼ ￼
	•	Data via React Query; no ad-hoc fetch in components (except PlotlyFigure).  ￼
	•	Plotly charts rendered from backend JSON; no client-side figure construction.  ￼
	•	Accessible labels, keyboard focus, and responsive layout verified.


### Artistic direction

Here’s a Design & Artistic Direction (DA) proposal for your new football analytics web app — something that feels modern, credible for data, and engaging for sports fans, while adapting to desktop and mobile.

⸻

🎨 Artistic Direction: “Data-driven Football”

Moodboard Keywords
	•	Modern sports tech
	•	Minimal + bold accents
	•	Data clarity first, entertainment second
	•	Dynamic but not cluttered

Color Palette
	•	Primary: Deep Navy (#0B1B3F) — trust, contrast for bright data
	•	Accent 1: Electric Green (#00FF88) — highlight performance, ties to “pitch” color
	•	Accent 2: Bright Orange (#FF6B35) — for alerts, top players, action buttons
	•	Backgrounds: Off-white (#F7F9FB) for sections, Dark Navy for charts
	•	Neutral text: Cool gray scale (#E5E7EB to #4B5563)

Typography
	•	Headings: Sans-serif, geometric (e.g., Poppins, Montserrat) → sporty & modern
	•	Body: Humanist sans-serif (e.g., Inter) → readability for data-heavy tables
	•	Use monospace only for metric codes or small numbers in compact charts.

Iconography & Graphics
	•	Simple, line-based icons (Lucide / Feather style) for league flags, positions, and actions
	•	No heavy realism — let charts & photos bring the realism

Imagery
	•	Player silhouettes in muted backgrounds for hero sections
	•	Background gradients with pitch lines subtly overlaid
	•	Action shots only in focused feature areas (e.g., player compare page)

⸻

🖥️ Page Layouts & UI Components

1. Home / Dashboard
	•	Hero section: “Find the best football players across Europe” with a call-to-action (“Explore Rankings”)
	•	League + season selector at the top
	•	Featured: “Top 5 players this week” card carousel
	•	CTA buttons: “Rankings” | “Compare players” | “Discover similar players”

2. Rankings
	•	Filter panel (left or top) with shadcn/ui Select, multi-league toggle
	•	Table view with sortable columns (metric, value, rank)
	•	Plotly bar chart view toggle (Top N visualization)
	•	On mobile → cards per player with mini bar indicator

3. Scatter / Compare Metrics
	•	Filter strip at top: X metric, Y metric, league, season, min minutes
	•	Full-width Plotly scatter chart with tooltip showing player & stats
	•	Mobile: collapsible filters, chart scales to width
	•	Highlight best-in-class with accent colors

4. Player Profile
	•	Header: player photo, team, league, key stats at glance
	•	Metric visualization: radar chart (Plotly) + performance bar list
	•	“Similar Players” grid — click to jump to their profile
	•	Historical trend mini-charts (performance over seasons)

5. Player Compare
	•	Two selectors (autocomplete search)
	•	Side-by-side bars for each metric
	•	Similarity score (big number, accent highlight)
	•	Option to share compare view link

⸻

📐 Design Patterns & Components
	•	Atomic Design: break down into atoms (buttons, tags), molecules (filter group, stat card), organisms (rankings table, player header)
	•	Responsive grid: 12-column desktop → stacked cards mobile
	•	Charts: dark background with bright accents for clarity; consistent legend placement
	•	Tables: zebra striping, sticky headers, compact on mobile
	•	Cards: rounded corners (16px+), subtle shadows, hover scale for interactivity

